@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+---
+id: dl-004-positional-non-lcs-array-diff-for-createpatch
+type: decision-log
+title: "Positional, non-LCS array diff for createPatch"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` needed to decide how to diff two JSON arrays found at the same location in
+`from` and `to`. RFC 6902 describes what a patch does when applied, not how to compute one, so there
+is real latitude here. The thorough answer — detecting insertions/deletions in the middle of an array
+via an LCS (longest common subsequence) alignment, so e.g. inserting one element at the front of a
+long array produces a single `add` rather than rewriting every shifted element — is asymptotically
+more expensive (and more code) than a positional diff, for a case that matters less than the object
+case the request specifically called out ("two versions differ in one small place").
+
+## Decision
+
+`diffArrays` compares `from` and `to` index-by-index up to their common length (recursing through the
+same `diffValues` used for object members, so a changed element one level down still produces a
+minimal nested patch rather than a wholesale `replace` of the element), then describes any leftover
+length difference as trailing operations: `add` at `"-"` for each extra element in `to` (append order,
+oldest-appended first), or `remove` from the highest leftover index down to the first one dropped (so
+earlier removals in the list never shift the index a later removal in the same list still needs).
+No LCS/edit-distance alignment is attempted, so an insertion or deletion in the middle of an array is
+described as a run of trailing `replace`s plus one trailing `add`/`remove`, rather than a single `add`
+or `remove` at the insertion point.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Appending to, truncating, or changing elements in place in an array — including nested changes
+  inside an unmoved element — all produce a minimally-sized patch, matching the request's "describe
+  what changed, and nothing more" for the common cases.
+- A middle insertion/deletion in a large array produces a patch proportional to the number of elements
+  after the insertion point, not to 1; a caller with that access pattern on large arrays would need a
+  different (LCS-based) implementation. Not attempted here, consistent with this codebase's existing
+  preference for the direct reading of each RFC over more elaborate algorithms (e.g. dl-002's
+  remove-then-add `move`) and the project having no runtime dependency to reach for one.
+- `createPatch`'s object-member diff (`diffObjects`) has no equivalent ambiguity: RFC 6902 member names
+  are opaque keys, not positions, so there is exactly one correspondence between a member of `from` and
+  the "same" member of `to` — the matching key — and no analogous alignment question arises there.
